L000242	\section{One-mass pentagon functions in the decay channel}
L000243	\label{sec:pentagon-functions}
L000244	
L000245	A complete basis of special functions sufficient to represent any one- and two-loop five-particle finite remainder with a single external massive particle was constructed in Refs.~\cite{Chicherin:2021dyp,Abreu:2023rco}, together with an efficient numerical implementation.
L000246	These basis functions, known as (one-mass) pentagon functions, are of polylogarithmic type, i.e.~they can be represented as iterated integrals of logarithmic one-forms~\cite{Abreu:2022mfk}.
L000247	
L000248	The construction in Refs.~\cite{Chicherin:2021dyp,Abreu:2023rco} is however performed in the production channel with two incoming massless particles.
L000249	On the one hand, the analytic representation of the basis elements used for numerical evaluation is valid only in that region.
L000250	On the other, the basis elements themselves are chosen to minimize the occurrence of spurious singularities --- singularities of the integration kernels where the functions themselves remain finite --- inside the production region.
L000251	
L000252	In this work, we construct a dedicated set of pentagon functions for the decay channel, where all four massless particles are in the final state.
L000253	The latter is defined by the following inequalities:
L000254	\begin{align}
L000255	  p_1^2 >0 \,, \quad
L000256	  p_1 \cdot p_i < 0  \,, \quad
L000257	  p_i \cdot p_j > 0  \,, \quad \Delta_5 <0 \,,
L000258	\end{align}
L000259	for $2\le i < j \le 5$, where all momenta are outgoing, $p_1$ is the momentum of the decaying particle, and $\Delta_5$ is the determinant of the Gram matrix of $p_1$, $p_2$, $p_3$ and $p_4$.
L000260	
L000261	
L000262	
L000263	Following Refs.~\cite{Chicherin:2021dyp,Abreu:2023rco}, we solve the canonical differential equations (CDEs)~\cite{Henn:2013pwa} for the two-loop five-particle Feynman integrals obtained in Refs.~\cite{Abreu:2020jxa,Abreu:2021smk,Abreu:2023rco} in terms of iterated integrals.
L000264	From the space of solutions, we construct a graded basis of functions and express the basis elements as one-fold path integrals over products of logarithms, dilogarithms, and logarithmic one-forms~\cite{Caron-Huot:2014lda}, evaluated along integration paths lying entirely within the decay region.
L000265	This construction relies on the values of the Feynman integrals at an arbitrary base point in the decay channel. The requirement that the symbol-level basis lifts to a function basis~\cite{Abreu:2023rco} imposes strong constraints, fixing most of these values. The remaining constants are determined through numerical evaluations with \texttt{AMFlow}~\cite{Liu:2017jxz,Liu:2022chg}, which also serves as an independent consistency check.
L000266	We further crosscheck our results against an independent evaluation of all Feynman integrals at a random point in the decay channel obtained with either \texttt{AMFlow} or \texttt{DiffExp}~\cite{Hidding:2020ytt}.
L000267	
L000268	The arguments of logarithms and dilogarithms are drawn from the alphabet letters appearing in the CDEs~\cite{Duhr:2011zq}. Although the resulting one-fold integrals are well defined in the decay region, the integration paths may cross hypersurfaces where a letter vanishes, requiring an additional analytic treatment of the integrand prior to numerical evaluation~\cite{Chicherin:2021dyp}.
L000269	In this respect, the decay channel is simpler than the production channel: only letters that depend quadratically on the Mandelstam invariants can change sign in the production channel.
L000270	In particular, the degree-4 polynomial
L000271	\begin{align}
L000272	\begin{aligned}
L000273	& \Sigma_5 = (s_{12} s_{15} - s_{12} s_{23} - s_{15} s_{45} + s_{34} s_{45} + s_{23} s_{34})^2 \\
L000274	& \phantom{\Sigma_5 = } - 4 s_{23} s_{34}s_{45}(s_{34} - s_{12} - s_{15})\,,
L000275	\end{aligned}
L000276	\end{align}
L000277	and its permutations, which in Ref.~\cite{Abreu:2023rco} were shown to give rise to divergences within the production channel, have a definite sign throughout the decay channel.
L000278	All singularities in the decay channel are thus spurious, and the numerical implementation  follows straightforwardly the approach of Ref.~\cite{Chicherin:2020oor}, leading to improved performance and stability.
L000279	
L000280	The integration paths originate from the base point used in solving the CDEs.
L000281	We choose it to be symmetric under permutations of the final-state momenta.
L000282	This symmetry reduces the number of values needed to solve the CDEs and simplifies the iterated-integral representation of the solution by reducing the proliferation of distinct transcendental constants.
L000283	In contrast to the production channel, the permutation symmetry constrains the base point up to its normalization;
L000284	we choose it to be
L000285	\begin{align} \label{eq:X_0}
L000286	  p_1^2 = 1 \,, \quad \ s_{12} = s_{15} = \frac12 \,, \quad \ s_{23} = s_{34} = s_{45} = \frac16 \,,
L000287	\end{align}
L000288	where $s_{ij} = (p_i+p_j)^2$.
L000289	Interestingly, all letters that can vanish within the decay channel do so at the base point.
L000290	
L000291	The endpoint of the integration path, on the other hand, specifies the kinematic point at which the pentagon functions are evaluated.
L000292	As the production channel, also the decay one --- expressed in Mandelstam variables --- is not star-shaped: straight paths originating from the base point in Eq.~\eqref{eq:X_0} are not always sufficient to reach any point in the decay channel without leaving it.
L000293	In practice, we observe that two straight segments lying within the decay region suffice, with an intermediate point chosen according to the Monte Carlo strategy of Ref.~\cite{Chicherin:2021dyp}.
L000294	
L000295	
L000296	We make the numerical evaluation of the decay-channel pentagon functions publicly available through the \textsc{C++} library \texttt{PentagonFunctions++}~\cite{PentagonFunctions}.
L000297	Efficiency and stability of this evaluation routine are certified by the phenomenological study presented in this Letter.
L000298	Symbolic expressions of all relevant master integrals in terms of decay-channel pentagon functions, and expressions of the latter in terms of either iterated or one-fold integrals can be found in ancillary files~\cite{zenodo}.
L000299	Additionally, we provide the mapping between the production- and decay-channel pentagon functions.
L000300	Crossing from the production to the decay channel then simply amounts to replacing each production-channel pentagon function with the corresponding polynomial in the decay-channel pentagon functions.
L000301	Finally, we stress that these results include also the non-planar pentagon functions that do not appear in the LCA employed in this work.
